1
00:00:29,613 --> 00:00:33,492
There definitely is a lot
of Paris life and culture in Arcane.

2
00:00:33,575 --> 00:00:36,173
I mean, I don't think
it's a coincidence that, uh,

3
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:37,534
a lot...

4
00:00:38,747 --> 00:00:40,207
of Piltover looks like Paris.

5
00:00:42,334 --> 00:00:45,327
Things are designed to be functional,

6
00:00:45,504 --> 00:00:48,363
but also... to be a piece of art.

7
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,076
I think that's something
very much in the French, kind of, spirit.

8
00:00:54,209 --> 00:00:55,966
Yeah, so this venue...

9
00:00:56,723 --> 00:01:00,595
is actually where we had
Worlds a few years ago.

10
00:01:03,723 --> 00:01:05,589
It was cool
because it was really close to our studio,

11
00:01:05,808 --> 00:01:07,589
so, we sent some people from Fortiche.

12
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:10,771
It was something really special to
finally be able to share that with them.

13
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,991
I think they were fans
of the story and of the characters,

14
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:17,712
but you kind of also have to experience
the buzz in the world around the game.

15
00:01:20,914 --> 00:01:22,791
[IN FRENCH]
We saw straight away that this was...

16
00:01:23,709 --> 00:01:25,419
a world-wide game

17
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:27,703
and the chance to do
something really, really good.

18
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:33,499
[IN ENGLISH] For us,
Arcane was a big opportunity

19
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:36,263
to show to viewers that we are not

20
00:01:36,346 --> 00:01:39,349
only good for making trailers
and music videos.

21
00:01:40,434 --> 00:01:43,937
[IN FRENCH] It really starts with a
joyful,

22
00:01:44,207 --> 00:01:47,900
passionate intention to make a film,

23
00:01:47,983 --> 00:01:49,038
or a TV show,

24
00:01:49,401 --> 00:01:51,718
and not just marketing.

25
00:01:53,572 --> 00:01:55,128
[IN ENGLISH] If I'm confident about that?

26
00:01:55,616 --> 00:01:57,229
Uh...

27
00:01:57,409 --> 00:01:58,353
I don't know.

28
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:00,356
I hope...

29
00:02:01,163 --> 00:02:02,707
people are going to be surprised.

30
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:06,953
I think everybody on the project is...

31
00:02:07,484 --> 00:02:10,134
out to prove something
with what we're doing

32
00:02:10,714 --> 00:02:12,063
and I firmly believe that...

33
00:02:12,674 --> 00:02:15,470
you need to find someone
who's kind of willing to...

34
00:02:15,677 --> 00:02:18,502
bleed for this thing
they're trying to make.

35
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:20,874
I don't think, otherwise, you're going...

36
00:02:21,683 --> 00:02:23,937
to be bold, otherwise,
you're not going to have anything...

37
00:02:24,144 --> 00:02:25,128
where you'll risk something.

38
00:02:25,188 --> 00:02:28,774
And you have to risk something in order
to make something that makes an impact.

39
00:02:46,875 --> 00:02:50,629
A very, very competitive scene
here, at Fortiche,

40
00:02:50,712 --> 00:02:53,778
about what they call...
is it babyfoot right?

41
00:02:54,258 --> 00:02:55,530
-Babyfoot?
-WOMAN: Babyfoot, yes.

42
00:02:58,866 --> 00:03:00,498
CHRISTIAN: The suspense is killing me!

43
00:03:01,390 --> 00:03:02,568
WOMAN: It's killing us, too.

44
00:03:04,101 --> 00:03:05,593
Ah, there's the worthy goal!

45
00:03:09,022 --> 00:03:11,210
CHRISTIAN:
Basically, the idea for this trip is to...

46
00:03:11,335 --> 00:03:15,214
get into a room with the directors,
the animators, the editors,

47
00:03:15,276 --> 00:03:16,757
and just brainstorm a bit, together, like,

48
00:03:16,822 --> 00:03:18,467
"What would be
the coolest version of this scene

49
00:03:18,545 --> 00:03:19,743
or this episode?"

50
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:23,292
So, you're just worried
about it being too complex?

51
00:03:23,579 --> 00:03:26,874
AMANDA: My goals this whole time is,
I want to solve the story beats

52
00:03:26,957 --> 00:03:28,166
with the team in Paris.

53
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:32,104
It's just going to be great to be, like,
"Hey guys, here's all our awesome ideas,"

54
00:03:32,179 --> 00:03:35,132
and then they tell us
whether they can produce it or not

55
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:37,646
or steer us more
into something achievable.

56
00:03:37,942 --> 00:03:39,315
It's not done till it's done.

57
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:41,338
It's like the block of marble,

58
00:03:41,695 --> 00:03:42,506
chiseling away.

59
00:03:42,931 --> 00:03:45,601
And then occasionally,
you knock off an arm

60
00:03:45,684 --> 00:03:46,592
and have to start over.

61
00:03:46,986 --> 00:03:49,828
If we ever proceed with this scene
without taking in...

62
00:03:50,564 --> 00:03:52,830
that input from the directors here,
it's worse,

63
00:03:52,909 --> 00:03:55,954
because they're so talented,
they always find this creative spin.

64
00:03:56,653 --> 00:03:58,822
Pascal has this amazing eye...

65
00:03:58,906 --> 00:04:00,885
...for character designs,

66
00:04:01,283 --> 00:04:03,068
the models, the stylization.

67
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:08,821
[IN FRENCH] I don't know
if we could put in some camera shake?

68
00:04:10,690 --> 00:04:12,890
[IN ENGLISH] I'm just the man of shadows.

69
00:04:13,754 --> 00:04:15,299
Jerome, he's...

70
00:04:15,445 --> 00:04:17,631
the invisible wizard at Fortiche.

71
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:20,511
Today, I want to be at the lab

72
00:04:20,605 --> 00:04:23,858
to experiment with new things
for the future, with Riot.

73
00:04:24,275 --> 00:04:26,176
CHRISTIAN: I think,
when you think about the final look,

74
00:04:26,321 --> 00:04:29,031
that painterly look
that's integrated into 3D,

75
00:04:29,269 --> 00:04:33,157
the super subtle, nuanced camera work,
where everything feels

76
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:36,276
like it's hand-held
and really human and real,

77
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,884
a lot of that is Jerome.

78
00:04:39,029 --> 00:04:40,781
-De-aging with me, please.
-Okay.

79
00:04:40,864 --> 00:04:42,252
I want some de-aging.

80
00:04:42,491 --> 00:04:44,380
INTERVIEWER: Who is Arnaud?

81
00:04:44,929 --> 00:04:46,318
Who is Arnaud? Uh...

82
00:04:46,744 --> 00:04:48,462
Ah. Who is Arnaud?

83
00:04:48,539 --> 00:04:50,645
He tells us, "You know
what I want is a... Psh!

84
00:04:50,791 --> 00:04:53,001
what I want is a... [MIMICS EXPLOSION]
you know what?"

85
00:04:53,085 --> 00:04:54,473
-Who is Arnaud?
-Who is he?

86
00:04:56,755 --> 00:04:58,854
So, this is the Arnaud model.

87
00:04:59,341 --> 00:05:01,656
So, um, the hair is pretty detailed.

88
00:05:01,802 --> 00:05:03,002
Um...

89
00:05:04,346 --> 00:05:07,658
And then, he doesn't have any pants.

90
00:05:09,393 --> 00:05:10,657
And...

91
00:05:10,926 --> 00:05:14,295
ANIMATOR: They wanted to incorporate
the three directors in the show.

92
00:05:14,444 --> 00:05:15,925
Happy Progress Day!

93
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:20,032
When Arnaud has, like, a crazy idea,

94
00:05:20,737 --> 00:05:23,198
best thing you can do
is shut up and let him do his thing,

95
00:05:23,292 --> 00:05:26,722
because it tends to be
when the cool shit happens.

96
00:05:32,374 --> 00:05:35,085
I loved that they added them
as kids in there.

97
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:39,756
It was not something
we thought of in the room...

98
00:05:41,717 --> 00:05:45,128
...but it just added
this level of emotion between them.

99
00:05:46,346 --> 00:05:47,181
Honestly, it's like,

100
00:05:47,264 --> 00:05:50,225
"Oh, I feel like these characters
could be in love now."

101
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,959
This sequence is in every way,
technically, artistically...

102
00:06:04,198 --> 00:06:06,489
It's, I don't know how to say that,

103
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:09,154
the masterpiece of the whole season.

104
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,581
CHRISTIAN: This particular image to me
is, like, the combination...

105
00:06:12,664 --> 00:06:14,678
You know, Hextech is art and science,

106
00:06:14,917 --> 00:06:18,253
and I think that's exactly
what I feel the Fortiche depiction is.

107
00:06:18,837 --> 00:06:21,381
I feel the science,
I feel the power, I feel the energy,

108
00:06:21,465 --> 00:06:22,549
but I also feel the beauty.

109
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:27,118
ARNAUD [IN FRENCH]:
What I like about Fortiche is

110
00:06:27,513 --> 00:06:30,724
that we breathe life
into the original vision

111
00:06:30,807 --> 00:06:33,393
and everyone kind of adds their stone...

112
00:06:34,019 --> 00:06:36,104
-...to the building.
-...to the building, as we say.

113
00:06:36,996 --> 00:06:37,732
So...

114
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:42,578
[IN ENGLISH] So, basically,

115
00:06:42,578 --> 00:06:46,168
this board is all the steps
that happen at Fortiche.

116
00:06:46,669 --> 00:06:50,651
After we receive the script,
we go to the design phase,

117
00:06:51,013 --> 00:06:53,021
which basically is doing design

118
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:55,582
for characters, props and backgrounds.

119
00:06:57,084 --> 00:06:59,139
[IN FRENCH] In this case,

120
00:06:59,503 --> 00:07:03,590
this boat here was made by Viktor

121
00:07:03,954 --> 00:07:08,179
and actually putting this boat
in the scene is a way

122
00:07:08,387 --> 00:07:12,391
of showing that, even as a child,
Viktor was a genius.

123
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,960
[IN ENGLISH]
Then, everything goes to storyboard.

124
00:07:22,109 --> 00:07:25,343
In the storyboard department,
we take the scripts

125
00:07:25,343 --> 00:07:28,418
and we work with the directors.

126
00:07:28,822 --> 00:07:30,441
We try to build something

127
00:07:30,441 --> 00:07:33,226
that is not there yet in the script.

128
00:07:34,233 --> 00:07:37,605
But there's also editing,
who are working at the same time

129
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,335
in order to really find
the good timings for the boards.

130
00:07:41,753 --> 00:07:42,754
As an editor,

131
00:07:42,838 --> 00:07:46,133
my job is to assemble
those storyboards together

132
00:07:46,216 --> 00:07:47,718
into what we all an animatic.

133
00:07:49,178 --> 00:07:51,806
And then, while that's happening,
you're recording

134
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:53,682
the dialogue with the talent.

135
00:07:54,266 --> 00:07:55,946
I love a good conundrum.

136
00:07:56,894 --> 00:07:59,263
Hammer forging is such a delicate art.

137
00:07:59,631 --> 00:08:01,481
Shimmer is rampant in the Undercity,

138
00:08:01,565 --> 00:08:04,382
attacks at the Hexgates
and in the Academy square.

139
00:08:04,621 --> 00:08:08,051
Yeah, but I-I said I already called it.
You can't do... I called dibs!

140
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:10,741
We get voices from the actors

141
00:08:10,824 --> 00:08:12,734
and then we cut them with sound effects

142
00:08:12,734 --> 00:08:13,955
and temp music

143
00:08:14,131 --> 00:08:16,580
in order to see the movie
and see how it works.

144
00:08:22,419 --> 00:08:25,047
We're watching
full-on storyboard animatics

145
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:27,019
with the voice actors in there.

146
00:08:27,506 --> 00:08:29,134
VI: Vander learns none of this.

147
00:08:29,218 --> 00:08:31,762
MYLO: No worries there.
Powder took care of the evidence.

148
00:08:31,845 --> 00:08:33,347
I tried, okay?

149
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:35,925
So, we were able to watch the whole season

150
00:08:36,195 --> 00:08:40,354
from start to finish before we animated
the rest of the episodes.

151
00:08:40,938 --> 00:08:41,940
You did this?

152
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:45,838
I mean, honestly, on every aspect,

153
00:08:45,910 --> 00:08:47,965
we were afraid
of this moment in the episode,

154
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:49,672
because it's not always easy

155
00:08:49,672 --> 00:08:53,553
to, like, get these really raw,
emotional performances

156
00:08:53,605 --> 00:08:56,305
from really young actors.

157
00:08:56,347 --> 00:08:59,488
And so, Mia Jenness,
who we cast for Powder...

158
00:08:59,665 --> 00:09:01,244
I remember going into the VO session

159
00:09:02,042 --> 00:09:02,942
and being, like, "All right,

160
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:07,175
today we're going to make
an 11-, 12-year-old girl cry.

161
00:09:07,631 --> 00:09:09,650
Um… let's see how that goes."

162
00:09:09,776 --> 00:09:11,145
MAN: And rolling on this.

163
00:09:14,137 --> 00:09:15,203
She left me...

164
00:09:18,392 --> 00:09:21,870
She is NOT my sister anymore.

165
00:09:25,414 --> 00:09:27,728
I remember all of us
in the booth just, like,

166
00:09:28,402 --> 00:09:30,000
being shocked by how good she is

167
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,376
and just...

168
00:09:33,448 --> 00:09:37,717
being in tears at the end of the... takes.

169
00:09:37,793 --> 00:09:38,926
MAN: And I think we got it.

170
00:09:39,538 --> 00:09:40,560
Yay!

171
00:09:40,706 --> 00:09:42,073
I got the job when I was 10

172
00:09:42,833 --> 00:09:44,276
and I'm almost 16,

173
00:09:44,918 --> 00:09:45,830
so it's been a while.

174
00:09:46,628 --> 00:09:48,131
Why did you leave me?!

175
00:09:48,463 --> 00:09:49,965
Because you're a jinx!

176
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:53,135
No... No... No, Vi, please...

177
00:09:53,591 --> 00:09:56,096
To get into that place of vulnerability...

178
00:09:56,805 --> 00:09:57,755
was hard for me.

179
00:09:58,819 --> 00:10:01,435
Vi! Vi, come back! Please, come back!

180
00:10:01,518 --> 00:10:04,240
Please, Violet, I need you! Please...

181
00:10:04,513 --> 00:10:08,630
I have a little brother
who is my best friend in the entire world.

182
00:10:08,775 --> 00:10:12,207
We get upset when we don't see each other
for, like, more than three days.

183
00:10:12,863 --> 00:10:15,575
I can't imagine a world
where he isn't in it.

184
00:10:15,782 --> 00:10:17,307
-Where's the...?
-Oh, you're back!

185
00:10:18,535 --> 00:10:20,723
Thinking about what that would be like

186
00:10:20,916 --> 00:10:25,285
if something had happened that made it
so that we couldn't bounce back,

187
00:10:25,759 --> 00:10:29,346
next thing I knew,
it was all just coming to the surface.

188
00:10:29,691 --> 00:10:31,598
I only wanted to help...

189
00:10:32,554 --> 00:10:33,959
I only wanted to help.

190
00:10:34,384 --> 00:10:35,677
I only wanted to help!
I only wanted to-

191
00:10:35,677 --> 00:10:37,566
I told you to stay away.

192
00:10:38,430 --> 00:10:41,748
When it comes to siblings,
they're your blood.

193
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:44,272
And when you don't have family,
what do you have?

194
00:10:47,564 --> 00:10:48,693
You've got a good heart.

195
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:50,621
Don't ever lose it.

196
00:10:51,818 --> 00:10:53,999
No matter how the world tries
to break you.

197
00:10:55,906 --> 00:10:57,950
[IN FRENCH]
When we heard the voices, we were, like,

198
00:10:58,033 --> 00:11:01,828
"Ah, maybe we could have
something really...

199
00:11:01,912 --> 00:11:05,374
special, something much more subtle

200
00:11:05,457 --> 00:11:09,419
and much deeper in terms of acting."

201
00:11:12,673 --> 00:11:14,508
I always knew you'd come back.

202
00:11:14,719 --> 00:11:15,764
What's going on?

203
00:11:18,053 --> 00:11:19,721
When we heard the voices, we went,

204
00:11:19,805 --> 00:11:21,956
"Ah, we could lean
towards Scorsese instead."

205
00:11:28,856 --> 00:11:30,941
All the main characters
were really scary to cast.

206
00:11:31,024 --> 00:11:33,027
Because-
I mean, you're not just making a new character.

207
00:11:33,027 --> 00:11:33,664
It was, like,

208
00:11:34,027 --> 00:11:37,554
"Oh yeah, there's... whatever, six years of...

209
00:11:38,532 --> 00:11:42,119
a hundred gazillion, million people
who have very specific expectations

210
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:43,780
and opinions
about who these characters are.

211
00:11:44,954 --> 00:11:45,894
So don't **** it up."

212
00:11:47,332 --> 00:11:49,243
Wow, super boring.

213
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,224
Jinx took us the longest
of all the characters.

214
00:11:52,462 --> 00:11:55,978
I think we looked for the Jinx actress
for roughly three years.

215
00:11:56,717 --> 00:12:00,676
ALEX: She's so beloved
and her voice is so specific.

216
00:12:00,971 --> 00:12:04,751
For a while, we were, like,
"Can we find someone who can do this?"

217
00:12:05,225 --> 00:12:08,751
CHRISTIAN: The hard thing was
that we had to find someone who could do both...

218
00:12:08,897 --> 00:12:10,898
the intimate and quiet moments

219
00:12:10,981 --> 00:12:13,817
that are these
really vulnerable acting moments.

220
00:12:13,901 --> 00:12:16,113
But at the same time,
we also needed an actress

221
00:12:16,113 --> 00:12:19,376
who can also do the really loud moments
that Jinx is really known for.

222
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:22,743
And you typically can find
one or the other.

223
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:25,913
We were at a point where it was,
"We've been looking so long."

224
00:12:25,996 --> 00:12:28,999
We kind of went through
all of the LA catalogue of talent.

225
00:12:29,082 --> 00:12:30,649
"Let's just pick one and just go."

226
00:12:31,168 --> 00:12:33,913
And it was just, like, "No, we can't."

227
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:39,699
And, at some point, through the help
of some casting folks,

228
00:12:39,968 --> 00:12:41,129
we found Ella.

229
00:12:42,429 --> 00:12:42,984
Hi.

230
00:12:43,627 --> 00:12:44,306
And, I mean,

231
00:12:44,389 --> 00:12:46,487
it was like the Pearly Gates just opened.

232
00:12:47,768 --> 00:12:48,427
Well,

233
00:12:49,269 --> 00:12:50,419
look who it is.

234
00:12:51,605 --> 00:12:52,962
The Boy Savior!

235
00:12:54,650 --> 00:12:57,069
I felt this really
deep connection with her,

236
00:12:57,152 --> 00:12:59,823
because of empathy and compassion,
on compassionate grounds.

237
00:12:59,853 --> 00:13:00,753
Yes, she's...

238
00:13:01,365 --> 00:13:04,764
a terrible person,
she's done all these terrible things.

239
00:13:05,327 --> 00:13:07,162
But is she a terrible person?
I don't know.

240
00:13:07,246 --> 00:13:08,379
I started to realize, like...

241
00:13:09,456 --> 00:13:12,262
who's she's become,
this Jinx that she's become, is just a...

242
00:13:12,501 --> 00:13:13,621
deeply rooted,

243
00:13:13,621 --> 00:13:17,076
very difficult trauma response
that she's developed

244
00:13:17,214 --> 00:13:19,453
because she's gone through things
no one should have to go through.

245
00:13:20,634 --> 00:13:23,505
No... No, it was a mistake...

246
00:13:24,638 --> 00:13:29,059
[IN FRENCH] It's true,
the acting, I think, in Arcane, is

247
00:13:29,142 --> 00:13:32,479
much more advanced
than in most animated shows,

248
00:13:32,563 --> 00:13:35,807
because our desire was more to try...

249
00:13:37,985 --> 00:13:39,069
and really copy the actors,

250
00:13:39,152 --> 00:13:42,501
and sometimes, the casting even influenced

251
00:13:42,739 --> 00:13:46,076
the design of the characters
and their attitudes.

252
00:13:47,286 --> 00:13:49,830
[IN ENGLISH]
In parallel with the storyboard, we're going to do

253
00:13:49,913 --> 00:13:52,656
background
and character-props fabrication.

254
00:13:57,838 --> 00:14:00,966
At the beginning,
we work at the same time as animation,

255
00:14:01,049 --> 00:14:06,138
so it's sometimes difficult to know
exactly the position of the characters.

256
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,197
You can see it's 2D, in black and white.

257
00:14:13,896 --> 00:14:17,024
I'm not painting it
because there is Silco there.

258
00:14:17,941 --> 00:14:21,591
[IN FRENCH] What I like is the
evolution of the characters in the story.

259
00:14:21,892 --> 00:14:23,822
And she has a really big evolution.

260
00:14:25,949 --> 00:14:27,910
I think that's why
she's my favorite character.

261
00:14:27,993 --> 00:14:29,765
I like the fact
that she's a little bit crazy.

262
00:14:30,057 --> 00:14:31,227
But at the same time, she's...

263
00:14:32,289 --> 00:14:33,489
not that crazy,

264
00:14:34,208 --> 00:14:35,157
not that wicked.

265
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:41,049
[IN ENGLISH] So, we've got designs,
and from there,

266
00:14:41,109 --> 00:14:42,708
we just build it in 3D.

267
00:14:43,319 --> 00:14:44,104
Yeah.

268
00:14:44,487 --> 00:14:45,553
It's very simple...

269
00:14:46,322 --> 00:14:47,118
...to explain.

270
00:14:47,573 --> 00:14:50,218
So here, we made the Atlas Gauntlet

271
00:14:50,388 --> 00:14:53,268
and... the most difficult part was,

272
00:14:53,570 --> 00:14:56,774
there are a lot of tiny parts
that work together.

273
00:14:57,386 --> 00:14:59,839
So, before it gets
to the rigging department,

274
00:15:00,108 --> 00:15:02,343
we have to be sure it works first,

275
00:15:02,861 --> 00:15:06,689
like, all the tiny pieces you see here.

276
00:15:06,865 --> 00:15:11,121
It was engineered precisely
to work like that.

277
00:15:11,702 --> 00:15:16,390
So, it's part of the fun,
but it's part of the headache, too.

278
00:15:18,544 --> 00:15:23,131
It's actually really fun
to create all those intricate pieces,

279
00:15:23,131 --> 00:15:26,815
like the leg and feet of Viktor,

280
00:15:27,177 --> 00:15:31,288
all those weird, metallic parts
blending with the body.

281
00:15:31,807 --> 00:15:33,531
This was the design for the hands.

282
00:15:34,142 --> 00:15:36,137
Then, I modelled something like this.

283
00:15:36,687 --> 00:15:39,774
And then, I gave it
to the texture department

284
00:15:39,982 --> 00:15:43,070
and they painted it so it looks like that.

285
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:05,405
MATTHIEU: All the process is built
around the idea of bringing the design

286
00:16:06,175 --> 00:16:07,467
to the final image.

287
00:16:07,476 --> 00:16:08,477
And that's great,

288
00:16:08,540 --> 00:16:12,139
because the design
is just brought back in 3D

289
00:16:12,222 --> 00:16:14,976
and it's actually
what the designer thought of.

290
00:16:19,396 --> 00:16:23,275
[IN FRENCH] Here, what I'm showing you
is a rig, a character rig.

291
00:16:24,902 --> 00:16:29,198
It's really joints, fictitious bones,
that we put in place.

292
00:16:29,281 --> 00:16:34,454
So, it's pivot points that we can turn.

293
00:16:38,457 --> 00:16:41,502
We have such a strong team of animators

294
00:16:41,585 --> 00:16:44,922
that they'll find,
I don't know, three or four ideas

295
00:16:45,005 --> 00:16:47,009
to add life to the character.

296
00:16:49,468 --> 00:16:52,975
[IN ENGLISH]
And then, we go to the layout stage

297
00:16:53,347 --> 00:16:56,266
and they start to do the staging
and the camera movement.

298
00:16:57,768 --> 00:17:00,187
[IN FRENCH]
We try to translate the storyboard

299
00:17:00,270 --> 00:17:01,563
into 3D...

300
00:17:02,814 --> 00:17:06,944
and to get across the emotions
just with a camera,

301
00:17:07,027 --> 00:17:09,905
with the camera movements,
or the focal length we choose...

302
00:17:12,950 --> 00:17:14,702
to find nice framing...

303
00:17:17,162 --> 00:17:20,418
and to also find
the rhythm of the animation.

304
00:17:27,631 --> 00:17:30,342
[IN ENGLISH] And then it goes to
animation.

305
00:17:49,863 --> 00:17:52,573
[IN FRENCH] And then perhaps Vander...
He's going to go.

306
00:17:52,656 --> 00:17:55,993
He leaves and then…
He's actually fallen.

307
00:17:56,076 --> 00:17:58,371
You don't need to have a huge distance,
but just feel...

308
00:17:59,288 --> 00:18:04,127
[IN ENGLISH] If you go in a dictionary,
"animation" is giving life to something.

309
00:18:04,645 --> 00:18:09,132
Being animated is just feeling
the passion of something as well.

310
00:18:09,464 --> 00:18:13,754
And us, we are like the puppet masters,
the puppeteers.

311
00:18:27,608 --> 00:18:28,567
No motion capture.

312
00:18:28,650 --> 00:18:30,194
-INTERVIEWER: No motion capture?
-No.

313
00:18:31,028 --> 00:18:33,235
Please, let people know that.

314
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:36,651
-Why not?
-Well...

315
00:18:37,149 --> 00:18:38,192
Motion capture...

316
00:18:38,243 --> 00:18:43,146
is used when you want
to have some realistic animation...

317
00:18:44,041 --> 00:18:45,125
and only that.

318
00:18:48,629 --> 00:18:49,160
Look out!

319
00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:50,214
CAITLYN: Ah!

320
00:18:54,384 --> 00:18:58,889
MARTIAL: Keyframe animation, it pushes you
to understand what happens in the body,

321
00:18:58,972 --> 00:19:00,642
how everything works together

322
00:19:00,849 --> 00:19:05,466
to have, like, this feeling of natural
and believable animation.

323
00:19:05,883 --> 00:19:08,140
And also,
it's about the style of the show.

324
00:19:08,565 --> 00:19:12,027
It's semi-realistic,
but it's not fully realistic.

325
00:19:12,307 --> 00:19:15,030
We tried to keep
something really hand-crafted.

326
00:19:15,299 --> 00:19:16,366
Animating is, like,

327
00:19:17,157 --> 00:19:19,076
acting out the characters.

328
00:19:19,159 --> 00:19:20,860
It's about performance.

329
00:19:23,622 --> 00:19:25,583
When I saw the style of Arcane,

330
00:19:25,791 --> 00:19:28,460
it was something, like,
"I want to try this kind of animation,

331
00:19:28,544 --> 00:19:30,337
because it's really different from the...

332
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:33,131
cartoony style I used to be in."

333
00:19:33,215 --> 00:19:38,136
And after working so long in the industry,
I wanted to challenge myself.

334
00:19:42,349 --> 00:19:44,226
To be honest,
Ekko is the kind of character

335
00:19:44,309 --> 00:19:46,125
I would have loved to see
when I was a kid.

336
00:19:48,105 --> 00:19:48,784
Ekko?

337
00:19:50,172 --> 00:19:52,130
One of the first dialogues he has with Vi,

338
00:19:52,276 --> 00:19:55,218
I went with something
completely different from the board

339
00:19:55,654 --> 00:19:56,239
and...

340
00:19:57,114 --> 00:19:58,448
Bart was, like, "Oh,

341
00:19:58,532 --> 00:20:01,338
I hadn't thought about it in this way,
but it's interesting."

342
00:20:01,702 --> 00:20:03,036
And it was the same with Christian.

343
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,852
I tried something
like he was a little bit pissed

344
00:20:05,956 --> 00:20:09,346
and walking slower
and just making this face

345
00:20:09,710 --> 00:20:11,608
and giving a bit of attitude.

346
00:20:12,379 --> 00:20:16,403
Not like a snake,
but, like, this kind of movement to say,

347
00:20:16,478 --> 00:20:19,344
"Yeah, I'm the one controlling the stuff."

348
00:20:19,770 --> 00:20:21,506
BART: So in a shot like that,

349
00:20:22,055 --> 00:20:25,312
the animator,
when he was assigned the shot,

350
00:20:25,893 --> 00:20:28,729
he'd shoot himself
in order to get some references

351
00:20:28,812 --> 00:20:31,921
and that's what gives us
the realistic look

352
00:20:32,191 --> 00:20:34,419
and the live-action feel.

353
00:20:34,475 --> 00:20:38,614
We do work a lot with references.
We go in the reference room.

354
00:20:38,697 --> 00:20:40,430
We shoot ourselves, we try to...

355
00:20:41,055 --> 00:20:44,077
to pretend and be the character
and being the right emotion.

356
00:20:45,204 --> 00:20:46,936
Why aren't you playing with the others?

357
00:20:47,548 --> 00:20:49,345
Why aren't you playing with the others?

358
00:20:49,708 --> 00:20:52,669
The video references are really
something that helps you

359
00:20:52,753 --> 00:20:54,286
capture something natural.

360
00:20:55,839 --> 00:20:57,674
Sometimes, you've got a shot to do

361
00:20:57,758 --> 00:20:59,551
and you've got an idea in your head,

362
00:20:59,635 --> 00:21:02,596
and you can probably just go
straight into the computer

363
00:21:02,679 --> 00:21:05,101
and do your stuff, and it would work.

364
00:21:05,196 --> 00:21:08,393
But if you go into the ref room
and you start recording yourself,

365
00:21:08,477 --> 00:21:11,605
you may end up having
some little accident that you do

366
00:21:11,968 --> 00:21:13,315
that can be really interesting,

367
00:21:13,315 --> 00:21:15,497
like, "That's pretty cool,
let's dig into that."

368
00:21:20,571 --> 00:21:22,324
MYLO: Remind me
why we bother with this dump?

369
00:21:22,950 --> 00:21:25,714
AMARA: How can I say no
to such an auspicious offer?

370
00:21:36,547 --> 00:21:39,770
Do you feel like you're inhabiting
those different characters in a way?

371
00:21:40,133 --> 00:21:42,004
Well, you have to.

372
00:21:42,989 --> 00:21:46,736
When you see one second of an episode,

373
00:21:47,678 --> 00:21:49,414
an animator spent a day on it.

374
00:21:49,809 --> 00:21:54,430
So, if you work on an expression,
on a feeling

375
00:21:54,731 --> 00:21:56,756
on a shot that lasts for one second,

376
00:21:57,150 --> 00:21:59,736
you're going to spend a day
having that feeling in your mind,

377
00:21:59,744 --> 00:22:00,988
like, trying to reproduce it.

378
00:22:03,282 --> 00:22:06,368
It's not rare to have... you go around

379
00:22:06,451 --> 00:22:09,121
the animation department,
and you can see people going, like...

380
00:22:11,748 --> 00:22:15,642
Or just this face
where you're just acting what...

381
00:22:15,942 --> 00:22:17,734
That's the common thing in animation, really.

382
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:24,428
I really wanted to be
part of this sequence particularly.

383
00:22:24,511 --> 00:22:28,056
There are a lot of crazy sequences,
but this one was the one for me.

384
00:22:28,140 --> 00:22:31,248
And when I heard that I was working on it,
I was, like...

385
00:22:32,186 --> 00:22:33,140
It was like a dream.

386
00:22:35,063 --> 00:22:38,517
And I was really happy,
because I had the slow-motion moment

387
00:22:38,612 --> 00:22:41,780
and I had the opportunity
to work with my son

388
00:22:41,987 --> 00:22:45,470
to make the movement of young Ekko.

389
00:22:45,802 --> 00:22:48,340
He's nine years old, almost ten.

390
00:22:48,537 --> 00:22:52,876
We came here to shoot the scene where Ekko
is running slowly, in slow motion.

391
00:22:55,292 --> 00:22:57,401
It was so cool to share that with him.

392
00:22:58,420 --> 00:23:00,547
It was, like, a moment, father-and-son,

393
00:23:00,631 --> 00:23:03,050
and I hope he will remember it

394
00:23:03,133 --> 00:23:04,085
all his life.

395
00:23:14,063 --> 00:23:18,078
MAN: So only experimenting for now
with paintings.

396
00:23:22,486 --> 00:23:24,613
Uh, it's about to be tested.

397
00:23:25,573 --> 00:23:28,283
For the glitches,
we draw them directly on the computer...

398
00:23:30,866 --> 00:23:34,164
...and for this one, we're searching
for another kind of process.

399
00:23:35,165 --> 00:23:36,159
More realistic.

400
00:23:37,687 --> 00:23:39,916
MAN 2: 2D effects
are very specific at Fortiche,

401
00:23:39,957 --> 00:23:42,501
because, you know,
it's a Fortiche trademark,

402
00:23:42,714 --> 00:23:44,834
doing 2D effects on 3D animation.

403
00:23:46,009 --> 00:23:47,822
They're like traditional animators,

404
00:23:48,220 --> 00:23:50,755
doing effects frame by frame.

405
00:23:50,931 --> 00:23:54,543
[IN FRENCH] So, here we have...

406
00:23:55,811 --> 00:23:57,564
the power that Jayce creates
with his hammer.

407
00:23:59,605 --> 00:24:01,712
[IN ENGLISH] I'm drawing frame by frame

408
00:24:02,317 --> 00:24:03,603
and layer by layer.

409
00:24:06,071 --> 00:24:07,072
But sometimes,

410
00:24:07,155 --> 00:24:10,847
it's very difficult
to follow the camera movements,

411
00:24:10,993 --> 00:24:12,308
which are very interesting.

412
00:24:12,703 --> 00:24:14,997
This kind of shot is
really difficult to do,

413
00:24:15,080 --> 00:24:18,096
because the camera
is a little bit hard to follow.

414
00:24:18,242 --> 00:24:21,240
I think I worked
something like three weeks on it.

415
00:24:28,135 --> 00:24:31,858
[IN FRENCH] For ten years, we've made
the 2D effects for Riot's teasers.

416
00:24:34,808 --> 00:24:37,337
On the teaser...

417
00:24:37,337 --> 00:24:39,804
presentation for Jinx,
which was "Get Jinxed",

418
00:24:40,105 --> 00:24:42,691
we found the Fortiche style.

419
00:24:47,070 --> 00:24:50,782
[IN ENGLISH] I have this feeling
when I'm watching something in progress

420
00:24:50,866 --> 00:24:54,048
and we get to the animation phase,
and I'm looking at the animation, like,

421
00:24:54,286 --> 00:24:55,746
"How will you make this better?

422
00:24:55,829 --> 00:24:58,489
How is this scene going
to feel more immersive?

423
00:24:58,665 --> 00:25:02,002
How are they possibly going
to level this up?"

424
00:25:02,271 --> 00:25:07,616
When everything is done, animation,
backgrounds, simulation, 2D effects,

425
00:25:08,258 --> 00:25:11,496
we render it and we composite it.

426
00:25:13,222 --> 00:25:15,053
We've come to the end of the process

427
00:25:15,053 --> 00:25:20,597
and we literally just take all
of what the other departments have created

428
00:25:21,053 --> 00:25:22,502
and we put it together.

429
00:25:25,943 --> 00:25:28,820
Because the background is painted,
there is actually no light

430
00:25:28,904 --> 00:25:29,988
used for the characters.

431
00:25:30,072 --> 00:25:33,368
We have to adapt the characters
to the background.

432
00:25:33,700 --> 00:25:35,143
So when we see, like,

433
00:25:35,143 --> 00:25:39,373
a red light on Sevika,
and it changes to the green light

434
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:41,458
when she goes further, actually,

435
00:25:41,946 --> 00:25:43,418
technically, nothing is there.

436
00:25:43,502 --> 00:25:46,631
We have to create everything
from what we see in the background.

437
00:25:47,589 --> 00:25:49,646
So yeah, we go from here...

438
00:25:53,220 --> 00:25:53,879
to here.

439
00:25:59,810 --> 00:26:01,562
Compositing at Fortiche is one of their

440
00:26:01,645 --> 00:26:04,127
signature crafts, and it's just...

441
00:26:04,857 --> 00:26:07,067
a straight mystery box for me.

442
00:26:08,485 --> 00:26:09,560
How do they paint the light?

443
00:26:10,404 --> 00:26:11,375
I cannot tell you.

444
00:26:13,866 --> 00:26:16,863
It's not that I'm holding back anything,
but I...

445
00:26:17,661 --> 00:26:20,291
Some of the process,
I don't even understand.

446
00:26:22,684 --> 00:26:24,060
I don't know if I can show you.

447
00:26:24,102 --> 00:26:25,043
INTERVIEWER: The secret sauce.

448
00:26:25,043 --> 00:26:26,695
Yes, it's kind of secret.

449
00:26:31,082 --> 00:26:33,375
The way to light it, it's different.

450
00:26:33,552 --> 00:26:34,845
It's...

451
00:26:36,263 --> 00:26:38,913
a special Fortiche way.

452
00:26:40,851 --> 00:26:43,158
But it's so simple,
when you know the trick,

453
00:26:43,645 --> 00:26:44,646
you say, "Ah, ****."

454
00:26:46,857 --> 00:26:47,790
"So simple."

455
00:26:49,526 --> 00:26:50,917
It's not a magical trick,

456
00:26:51,061 --> 00:26:54,670
it's just artists and people
who are doing beautiful things.

457
00:26:58,340 --> 00:27:00,839
The real magic is in there.
Everywhere!

458
00:27:15,730 --> 00:27:18,921
THOMAS: Because of their handcrafted-ness
and the painter-y look,

459
00:27:19,347 --> 00:27:21,850
it gives this... timeless look
that I think...

460
00:27:21,934 --> 00:27:23,477
even when the technology evolves,

461
00:27:23,560 --> 00:27:26,281
that this will still feel... lasting.

462
00:27:26,364 --> 00:27:29,858
In the same way I can go back
and watch the Disney animations

463
00:27:29,942 --> 00:27:33,113
like Bambi or...

464
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:36,468
Cinderella, and... it's timeless.

465
00:27:36,657 --> 00:27:37,500
It doesn't age,

466
00:27:37,583 --> 00:27:40,881
because... it's handcrafted.

467
00:27:41,119 --> 00:27:43,114
PASCAL: Nothing is round in the show.

468
00:27:43,571 --> 00:27:45,924
If you have a glass,
if you see this glass,

469
00:27:46,227 --> 00:27:47,108
it's not round,

470
00:27:47,626 --> 00:27:50,161
because a drawing is not perfect.

471
00:27:50,337 --> 00:27:51,787
ARNAUD: If you see the eyes,

472
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:55,073
the pupil is not round.

473
00:27:55,466 --> 00:27:56,593
PASCAL: Except the gemstone.

474
00:27:57,077 --> 00:27:59,012
This is the only moment

475
00:27:59,012 --> 00:28:01,292
where there is a sphere, a perfect sphere.

476
00:28:06,179 --> 00:28:11,024
[IN FRENCH] So,
the idea is kind of to have each frame

477
00:28:11,108 --> 00:28:16,071
exceed the quality that you can produce
with classic 2D animation.

478
00:28:16,947 --> 00:28:18,490
And 3D lets us do that.

479
00:28:18,574 --> 00:28:21,201
And it's also to make sure every frame,

480
00:28:21,285 --> 00:28:23,203
every time you pause it, it's...

481
00:28:25,163 --> 00:28:27,709
a bit of concept art,
a beautiful illustration.

482
00:28:43,682 --> 00:28:45,350
There's just storytelling happening

483
00:28:45,434 --> 00:28:47,060
at every phase and every level.

484
00:28:47,393 --> 00:28:50,480
MAN: Alex, Alex on the other side of the
ocean, good morning.

485
00:28:50,564 --> 00:28:51,213
Good morning.

486
00:28:51,606 --> 00:28:53,716
So, Ray, I think that was
a great first read.

487
00:28:54,234 --> 00:28:56,529
Can the second violins play that
a little bit softer?

488
00:28:59,323 --> 00:29:01,364
AMANDA: I've never worked on a show
before where...

489
00:29:01,613 --> 00:29:04,360
the musicians are
such an integral part of everything.

490
00:29:05,078 --> 00:29:07,988
CHRISTIAN: This is the Music team.
So, they wrote all the songs.

491
00:29:08,582 --> 00:29:12,458
They've been spending, what, three,
four years on the music for this show, like...

492
00:29:13,128 --> 00:29:16,317
